<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Cecilio del Valle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1780">1780</a>–<a href="/years/1834">1834</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Cecilio del Valle (1780–1834) was a Honduran lawyer, journalist and politician who is credited with drafting the Central American declaration of independence of 15 September 1821. A leading figure in the politics of the federation, he won the presidential election of 1834 but died before he could take office, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a> was re-elected in his place.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-cecilio-del-valle.jpg" alt="José Cecilio del Valle" width="320" height="365" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artist: Vilardell scanned by:JVC3ETAUploaded in black and white by Kes47 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Del_Valle_blanco_y_negro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Cecilio Díaz del Valle was born on 22 November 1780 in the village of Choluteca, near the Choluteca River, in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a>, now Honduras. His parents, Jose Antonio del Valle and Gertrudis Díaz del Valle, came from distinguished Spanish families of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, and their wealth lay mainly in livestock estates. Because Tegucigalpa lacked good schools, the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> when he was nine.</p><p>There he attended the University of San Carlos, taking a bachelor's degree in 1794, a degree in civil and canon law in 1799 and a licentiate in law in 1803; his mentor was Father José Antonio Liendo y Goycochea. He came to be nicknamed the Wise and was known as a moderate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Valle was elected mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> in 1821. He wrote the Act of Independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> that year but did not sign it, which remains a matter of controversy, though most historians agree he was not supposed to sign. When Central America joined the Mexican Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> in 1822, he sat in the Mexican Congress as a representative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Imprisoned with other deputies on 27 August 1822 on charges of conspiracy, he was released in February 1823 and appointed foreign minister of the Mexican government.</p><p>Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> in January 1824, he joined the second triumvirate governing the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. In 1825 he ran for president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-jose-arce">Manuel José Arce</a> and apparently won more votes, but Congress sent the contest to a second round and declared Arce the winner. Valle then devoted himself to writing. In 1830 he declined President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a>'s offer of the embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or the vice-presidency. He corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremy-bentham">Jeremy Bentham</a>, who sent him a lock of his hair.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died on 2 March 1834 on the road from the farm La Concepción to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>, where he was to be treated for an illness. The government decreed three days of mourning and bells tolled throughout the republic, for he was respected by Liberals and Conservatives alike. Honduras awards a medal for distinguished service in his name, a university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegucigalpa">Tegucigalpa</a> bears his name, his birthday on 22 November is a holiday, and his portrait has appeared on the 100 Lempira banknote since 1951.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Cecilio del Valle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Choluteca, in what is now <a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.31,-87.2&amp;year=1815" aria-label="Choluteca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2159 1668 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of José Cecilio del Valle"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2609 1938h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2609 1938h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.31,-87.2&amp;year=1815">What was here in 1815? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
